No. I think I know what my hon. friend has in mind. The legislation having to do with fair wages and the eight-hour day applied to public works and contracts. The order in council I have just read applied to Dominion government employees, while the act with respect to fair wages and the eight hour day for labour employed on public works of the Dominion of Canada, which is chapter 20-21, George V, assented to on May 30, 1930, provided:
His Majesty, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate and House of Commons of Canada, enacts as follows:-
1.	This act may be cited as The Fair Wages arid Eight Hour Day Act, 1930.
2.	In this act, the expression "minister" means the Minister of Labour.
3.	(1) Every contract made hereafter with the government of Canada for construction, remodelling, repair or demolition of any work shall be subject to the following conditions respecting wages and hours:-
(a)	all persons in the employ of the contractor, sub-contractor, or of any other person doing or contracting to do the whole or any part of the work contemplated by the contract shall be paid such wages as are generally accepted as current from time to time for competent workmen in the district in which the work is being performed for the character or class of work in which they are respectively engaged; provided that wages shall in all cases be such as are fair and reasonable;
(b)	The working hours of persons while so employed shall not exceed eight hours per day except in such special cases as the Governor in Council may otherwise provide, or except in cases of emergency, as may be approved by the minister.
(2) The provisions of this section shall not apply to persons employed in the fabrication or manufacture of materials, supplies or equipment for iise in the work contemplated where such fabrication or manufacture is carried on in any established plant or factory other than a plant or factory established for the purposes of the work contemplated.
4.	The wages and hours of all workmen employed by the government of Canada on such works as are described in section three, and who are excluded from the operation of the Civil Service Act, shall be those set forth in paragraphs fa) and (b) of section three.
5.	(1) The Governor in Council, on the recommendation of the minister, may make regulations with regard to wages and hours herein provided for and without limiting the generality of the foregoing may provide by regulation for,-
(a)	the method of determining what are current or fair and reasonable wages and the preparation and use of schedules of rates relating thereto;
(b)	rates of wages -for overtime;
(c)	classifications of employment or work;
(d)	the publication and posting of wage schedules;
(e)	payment of wages to employees in case of default by the contractor or .other party charged with such payment and recovery thereof from such contractor or other party;
(f)	the keeping of proper books and records and the examination of the same by government officers;
(g)	persons who may be employed on works referred to in this act;
(hi the subletting of contracts;
(i)	the penalties to be imposed for breaches of the provisions of this act or regulations made hereunder ;
(j)	generally for the due enforcement of the provisions of the act and regulations.
. (2) All regulations made under this act shall fifteen days after the date of the first publication thereof in the Canada Gazette have the same force and effect as if they had been included herein.
It will be seen that the Dominion government has gone about as. far as it is possible to go in a legislative way; the problem is how to get the provinces to carry out their obligations under the treaty. I do not believe
1718	COMMONS
Treaty of Versailles-Eight Hour Day
that Canada because of its peculiar constitution desires to evade its treaty obligations. There is only one province which (has put into effect the eight-hour day-the province of British Columbia, where my good friend (Mr. Mackenzie) comes from. When I was a member of the Ontario legislature in 1924 I moved a resolution and secured its passage, after it had been amended on motion of the premier of the day, to provide for an eight-hour day in all private and public undertakings. The motion passed tfhe legislature but up to the present time has not been implemented in a general way. That is the result in the province of Ontario which is not considered to be at all backward.
This treaty was signed in 1919 and we have now reached 1931 without any action being taken. It is time the Dominion government as a signatory on behalf of the whole of Canada should take the necessary steps to see that our treaty Obligations are carried out. While this resolution which I have the honour to move this evening does not suggest what steps t'he government should take, hon. members will realize that there are many ways by which the desired result can be obtained. One is by the calling of a conference between the Dominion and the provinces and pointing out that inasmuch as Canada has certain treaty obligations they as provinces should implement them so far as their jurisdiction will permit. If by chance any .province or provinces still desire to remain in a backward state and thus take advantage of other provinces which are more progressive, then I think it is the duty of the Dominion government to ask for an amendment to the British North America Act to enable it to carry out its treaty obligations in that regard. I hope that that step will not be necessary, and that the provinces will be unanimous in their desire to carry out these obligations.
Because of the position which he took when leader of the opposition, and again at the last session, perhaps the Prime Minister (Mr. Bennett), by attaching conditions to proposed tariff amendments, will take steps to implement these obligations. My motion leaves the matter wide open. I am not criticizing anyone because it may be felt that the action proposed by this resolution might have been taken long ago; but I do suggest that now is the time for the government to take appropriate steps to see to the fulfilment of our treaty obligations respecting hours of work.
